// An `extern fn`'s body is C, so no gate inside it can speak; `@heap` is how its declaration says the C
// touches the heap (KR-47). The call is then a fact like any allocation the compiler writes, and a `@noheap`
// region that reaches it is refused through the chain.
//
// Since KR-74 this is `@heap`'s ONLY job, and this fixture is the one that proves it still has one. The
// runtime's own externs carry no mark — the compiler reads `include/*.h` and derives what they do — so what
// is left for the attribute is exactly this: C the compiler cannot read. The symbol is therefore a made-up
// VENDOR name, not `malloc`/`calloc`: a libc allocator is refused whether or not it is marked (see
// noheap_unmarked_alloc), which would make this fixture pass for the wrong reason and leave the attribute
// itself untested — it did, briefly, while KR-74 was landing.
extern "<vendor_pool.h>";
@heap extern fn UnsafePtr vendor_block_alloc(usize n);
unsafe fn int32 grab() { UnsafePtr p = vendor_block_alloc(n: 4); return 0; }
@noheap fn int32 tick() { return grab(); }
fn int32 main() { return tick(); }
